WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Cedric was obviously part of the school when I was a student.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.000
So really my contact with Cedric came about as a student.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
He was in the building a lot. He was no longer teaching.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
He was involved in a number of high profile projects.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
So he redesigned the South Bank for instance and so on.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.000
He did a project for the South Bank and so on.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
But he was a character. He was a friend of Alvin's. He came to Jury's.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.615
So basically the wit and intelligence that Cedric had was something that you

00:00:34.615 --> 00:00:36.240
experienced firsthand.

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.800
It wasn't, I mean, it would be wrong to say he didn't play to the gallery.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.120
I think like Peter Cook, he was a character who played to the gallery.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.640
So whenever he spoke, he wanted the room to be listening.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.620
But I think he was also an incredible intelligence.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.120
So he would react to what you said or what was actually put in front of him.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.840
So my first meetings with him were always at juries.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.540
So he was there.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.560
He was pampered and spoilt by Alvin.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:15.120
So there's famous stories, you know, he'd be given a cigar as he came in

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.320
and a bottle of brandy would appear under his chair

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.940
and he would sit there and just fill his glass up and talk.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:30.060
So there were in those days quite a number of high profile juries as it were,

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.620
where people came from outside and so on, and you know,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.620
you as one as a student went to all these things to see what was said.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.380
And I think right from first year, I think going to these public juries,

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:48.977
you suddenly realise that Cedric was something that someone who you would go home

00:01:48.977 --> 00:01:51.420
and ponder about what he said.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.391
And I think what was always really important was that he never said anything in an

00:01:55.391 --> 00:01:56.020
academic way.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.520
He never said anything like a sort of professor or a teacher.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.480
He would sort of remark on something.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:08.693
It's rare to meet someone in the architectural environment in particular who is

00:02:08.693 --> 00:02:11.340
willing to talk about private things.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.440
You know, I think architects are quite facade-like.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:20.413
you know they wear black and they do this and they behave in a particular way and

00:02:20.413 --> 00:02:21.100
they only talk

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.792
about design um and i i think you know me personally you know i talk about fishing

00:02:26.792 --> 00:02:27.000
and

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.301
football and so on you know i mean i i i think you know we are all human and i think

00:02:32.301 --> 00:02:32.680
cedric

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:38.242
excelled at that he he he was a person who you felt warmth towards and i think he

00:02:38.242 --> 00:02:38.860
also felt

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:43.214
warmth and admiration which i think is part of this friendship because you were

00:02:43.214 --> 00:02:43.600
sharing

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.900
and understanding of what was around you,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:48.160
not simply because you were sort of architects

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.440
who were being sort of portrayed.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.600
Yeah, I think the uniqueness

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.200
was actually the level of sort of invention

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.320
and imagination that he could bring to the conversation,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.260
a level of unpredictability,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.780
a level of easy intelligence.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.900
So in many ways,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.420
when I say that a lot is written

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.264
and said and a lot is quoted and you know they're the archive of his tapes and I go

00:03:17.264 --> 00:03:17.820
to all these

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:22.254
things and I thoroughly enjoy them I don't I'm not not complaining about them but in

00:03:22.254 --> 00:03:22.940
a way I think

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:27.192
maybe the sign of friendship and this relationship that I'm talking about meant that

00:03:27.192 --> 00:03:27.800
he would say

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.845
something to you you you'd go home and think about it and then you'd forget what he

00:03:31.845 --> 00:03:32.040
said

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.538
so in a sense that's what what the true sign of you know someone actually affecting

00:03:36.538 --> 00:03:37.080
you in one

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.616
way or another was really about which wasn't ah I've got a quote you know he said

00:03:41.616 --> 00:03:42.400
this or he did

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.233
that or he made a reference. It was pretty much a capacity to see what you were

00:03:47.233 --> 00:03:47.600
trying

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:52.878
to do, to understand where you were coming from and then to actually add something

00:03:52.878 --> 00:03:53.200
which

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.042
possibly was well beyond what you had thought the possibilities were. And I think

00:03:58.042 --> 00:03:58.580
that made

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:09.866
it very important. He was a sort of extraordinary Englishman. I think that there

00:04:09.866 --> 00:04:11.700
were a series

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:20.189
of extraordinary Englishmen that affected me when I was young and when I was a

00:04:20.189 --> 00:04:21.060
student.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.174
I think John Somersom was one, Bob Evans was another one and Cedric was there. They

00:04:26.174 --> 00:04:26.420
were

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:34.502
all three completely different men. Somersom was the grandee, old gentleman and

00:04:34.502 --> 00:04:35.320
carer of

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.247
the CERN museum and so on and he came in his bicycle and his amazing tweed suits and

00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:39.340
so

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:48.991
Bob was a wonderful family man, working-class boy, plastic bag full of books and so

00:04:48.991 --> 00:04:49.340
on.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:54.640
And Cedric summed them both up in a funny way.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:58.100
So even the way Cedric looked was extraordinary.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.100
He looked like a politician in a sense, with his hairstyle.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:05.588
He looked like an old-fashioned Englishman with his thing all pulled back and his

00:05:05.588 --> 00:05:05.920
cigar.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.832
And then the stiff collar, the black silk tie, and the striped shirt, and then the

00:05:11.832 --> 00:05:12.120
hush

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.100
puppy shoes, you know, brothel creepers underneath.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.180
I mean, it's sort of complete.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:25.007
He was a mixture between, I suppose, the foot area was like a sort of geography

00:05:25.007 --> 00:05:25.800
teacher,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:31.552
the trousers were like school uniform, and the top was like a sort of bowler hat

00:05:31.552 --> 00:05:31.840
city

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:33.220
person.

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:38.135
And then in this extraordinary persona, you know, who used to come into a room, this

00:05:38.135 --> 00:05:38.720
incredible

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.940
intelligence would come out.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:45.080
So I think that he was unique, really unique.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.280
I mean, unique even to look at.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:53.820
In a sense he was, you know, his hair was always come back with sort of hair gel and

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:56.080
you know, et cetera, et cetera.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:06.665
And in many ways, I think, in a world of delirious New York, Manhattan transcripts,

00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:07.940
the things

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:09.020
which were happening.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:14.320
I mean, Bernard Chewmey was red-scarfed already.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.180
He would never be seen without it.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:21.840
Nigel Coates, who then came to teach me how to have his glasses and looked the part.

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:23.320
He was a fashion person.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.160
And Cedric was an anachronism.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:29.360
And I think that made him stand out a lot

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.460
because I think a lot of the things I then went on to do

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:36.500
had this Englishness that I'm talking about,

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:38.860
this sort of slight eccentricity.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:41.940
So when we moved on to do NATO,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.260
when I was in diploma school,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:44.840
when I was in Dip 10 and so on,

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.600
it was all kind of old-fashioned shirts

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.840
with the collar done up and old coats.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:52.220
and, you know, it was sort of some weird mixture

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.520
back to the 40s with sort of punk.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.400
And I'm not saying he was punk,

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.840
but I'm just simply saying he was quite extraordinary

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:00.620
in that sense, you know,

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.580
between the sort of studio jacket, the stiff collar.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:04.920
The stiff collar, you see, the stiff collar,

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.760
I'm still sort of bewildered by that.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:10.560
I mean, I don't know if he ever wore a stiff collar,

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:12.180
but they are really quite unpleasant.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.080
And he had one every day, and his tie on every day.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:23.599
So, you know, I think he was quite a striking figure within the whole process of

00:07:23.599 --> 00:07:25.880
what I was seeing at the AA.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:29.620
I think the other thing which was important is what I said before,

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:37.560
that I never got from Cedric this sense of an academic pretention or trajectory.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:42.980
So with Somerson, he was a great scholar and he was an absolutely charming man.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:46.140
with Bob Evans in his own quiet way,

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:48.020
he'd invite you around for tea.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.180
He was a true friend.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:50.880
I mean, I'd go to his house for tea and so on.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:52.740
You've got a sense, you know,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:54.760
God, this man's read all this and he's amazing

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:56.700
and he's teaching in Cambridge and he's written this

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:57.500
and he's written this essay.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:58.920
With Cedric, you never got that.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.580
Somehow, you didn't think, is he a great architect?

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:05.420
You sort of didn't decide.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.240
Is he a great draftsman?

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.140
He was a good drawer, actually.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:12.360
You sort of think, yeah,

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.960
but would I do lots of drawings of little ants?

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.320
He drew people everywhere, which is great.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:18.560
I used to love that.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.360
Every other architect did some pretentious sort of view.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:24.560
Is he a great writer?

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.440
In a way, there was one book on Cedric

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.360
that was published in those days,

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:29.840
but it's great.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:32.800
You've sort of read up about his projects and so on,

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:34.360
his seminal projects.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.280
I think that the other thing that's important to point out

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:38.840
is a lot of people say that this,

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:41.120
when I was a student in the 70s

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:47.692
well late 70s and 80s you know a lot of people referred to this as the heyday of the

00:08:47.692 --> 00:08:48.200
AA. Well

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:54.396
it wasn't. It wasn't sort of presented in that way. You didn't feel that. It was

00:08:54.396 --> 00:08:55.480
just a smaller

00:08:55.480 --> 00:09:00.651
place and there were some quite interesting people around. And it was more of a

00:09:00.651 --> 00:09:01.240
focus for

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:07.608
practitioners than it is now. So the bar was the bar. I mean people would come and

00:09:07.608 --> 00:09:08.600
hang around in

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:13.076
the bar and you'd meet you know famous architects as it were in the bar um but it

00:09:13.076 --> 00:09:13.960
wasn't a sort of

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:19.947
you know a place it never had the sort of sense of of of what i think i can only

00:09:19.947 --> 00:09:20.920
think so i've

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:25.157
never been to in that environment but you know it didn't have that sense of a sort

00:09:25.157 --> 00:09:25.880
of an american

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:29.896
university where the great professor walks in you know and then therefore you know

00:09:29.896 --> 00:09:30.680
everybody has to

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.820
sit up and listen it wasn't the aa was this sort of place where debates took place

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:36.760
and people came

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.880
because it was a fun thing to do.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.920
Cedric came, I think he had a great time.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:45.380
He didn't come here to pontificate, as I said.

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.400
I think he came here because he liked the discussion.

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.200
And, you know, so much so that I think the Cedrics

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.300
and the like in those days came to the parties.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:56.760
So we had lots of parties.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:00.400
You know, I think 1977 when I came

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.120
was the first AA carnival since the 40s

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:06.920
or maybe even pre-war.

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.140
So there was this big party, you know, 2,500 people,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.640
and there was a trapeze artist across Chinx Yard,

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:15.380
and, you know, there were custard fights in the bar and so on.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.320
You know, Cedric and people like Cedric would come to those parties.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.480
It was a sort of different atmosphere, I think.

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:26.180
So I don't think it was that, you know, that the AA attracted him.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:30.180
I think the AA was his sort of gravitational point,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.280
and he was part of that process.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:40.560
There is a key relationship between what I call physical and social structures,

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.920
i.e. that space is made up of the relationship between those two things.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:49.300
So therefore I think the Fun Palace sort of in a sense sums that up.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:55.780
It suggests the idea that space is never just a space that gets inhabited.

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:57.760
So it's a bit like the comment I was saying about the AA.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:59.560
The AA didn't exist and Cedric came to it.

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.000
The AA and Cedric were one.

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:05.929
So I think it begins to suggest that. I think in the sort of bit that I don't think

00:11:05.929 --> 00:11:06.760
is so relevant

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:11.627
is the idea of flexibility. I think another one that people look at is the idea of

00:11:11.627 --> 00:11:12.280
performance

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.524
or event. I don't think that's so interesting either. I think it's much more to do

00:11:15.524 --> 00:11:15.840
with the

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.822
idea of seeing something working, that has people in it. It's a sort of social

00:11:20.822 --> 00:11:22.100
structure that works

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.333
with a physical structure. I think that's very interesting. I think that the

00:11:26.333 --> 00:11:27.280
Thinkbelt project

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:31.120
is also interesting for that reason. It's talking about a system, you know, it's

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.660
actually talking about education in a different way and that's terrific. So

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.840
that is interesting to me today because I think maybe architects should be

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:41.620
thinking at that level. A lot of architects are thinking on a very banal

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:47.280
level of, you know, still form-making even though, you know, it's not talked about

00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:56.080
at this very day, you know, on whatever it is today, the 8th of May 2014.

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.540
I think there's still an obsession with this making of objects.

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.440
And I think both of those, of Cedric's projects,

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:09.780
were basically not really talking about the object.

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:13.120
They were talking about the relationship of the object

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:18.960
to the context within which they sat.

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:20.920
And that context was actually a social context,

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.080
an economic context, and a political context.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:29.740
I think that in terms of the grittiness of how the projects were presented,

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:36.100
I think that that light-hearted attitude that he had, which I mentioned before,

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:40.100
possibly would be out of place today,

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:47.538
because someone would make those two projects a lot more sort of theorising,

00:12:47.538 --> 00:12:49.120
political, strong.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:53.280
they'd be saying, no, no, this is the equivalent of the open university and so on.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:55.860
Whereas Cedric was, again, in that playfulness,

00:12:56.060 --> 00:12:59.800
actually suggesting things that foretold the future.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:05.060
Yes, I think Cedric's emphasis on enabling, I think, is very, very important.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:12.240
I think, possibly, it's something that he never articulated

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.480
beyond the realm of architecture.

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:19.080
and I think it's something that is very close to my heart.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:24.880
So I think the idea of an architect not simply being a sort of form maker

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:27.740
or an object maker is very, very important.

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:34.380
So, but as far as my experience with Cedric was concerned,

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:40.140
I never really saw him becoming sort of involved at the bigger scale of urbanism

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:45.440
or the bigger scale of politics or at the bigger scale of sort of the,

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:51.120
politics that govern the making of space. It's not a conversation I ever had with

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:56.100
him or ever heard him talk about. So he did do these big projects for instance

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:00.180
he did the project for the South Bank where he kind of sort of he created

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:03.480
these whole new spaces you know the whole thing became much more of a public

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:09.060
space. But whether he was saying something about the politics of London

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:14.160
or about cities and so on I'm not so sure. So I think enabling he was he was

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:20.790
very intelligent about the idea that an architect doesn't have to resort to object

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:21.760
making. So I

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:26.906
think that was his vision of enabling and I think maybe everything that he did

00:14:26.906 --> 00:14:28.160
including his funny

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:32.130
stories of let's say dinner with plates that you could see through to the floor and

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:32.800
so on you know

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:37.249
all these were like I think this is there's some some some extraordinary thing that

00:14:37.249 --> 00:14:38.000
he did I think

00:14:38.000 --> 00:14:41.456
he served food with see-through plates so that people could see their knees while

00:14:41.456 --> 00:14:41.840
they were

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:46.176
eating on a glass table, you know, and it was all laid out. I think there's a video

00:14:46.176 --> 00:14:46.960
actually of it,

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:51.544
so that, you know, when you finished eating your course you could then see your

00:14:51.544 --> 00:14:52.320
knees and everybody

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:56.168
else's knees on the table, you know, and he sort of, he thought that was amusing,

00:14:56.168 --> 00:14:56.880
you know. But I

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.155
think, you know, that's a really architectural project in a sense, you know, so I

00:15:00.155 --> 00:15:00.640
think he was

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:05.287
doing it all the time, but I think his enabling was of a particular scale. I think

00:15:05.287 --> 00:15:06.080
it wasn't that

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:12.941
enabling of that on the one hand is fashionable of alternative practice, you know, I

00:15:12.941 --> 00:15:13.840
don't think

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:17.715
he was into that at all. I think what he was just simply saying is that the

00:15:17.715 --> 00:15:18.800
strictures of what an

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:25.237
architect is are not sacred, were not sacred, and therefore that's why he worked in

00:15:25.237 --> 00:15:26.400
his manner. And

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:31.938
I think actually a lot of his actions, as I was saying before, his being there, were

00:15:31.938 --> 00:15:32.400
just as

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:39.094
critical in his making of what his world was than the actual things he was drawing

00:15:39.094 --> 00:15:40.400
or writing upon.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:45.486
I think where it's difficult to pin it down is that I think a lot of people were

00:15:45.486 --> 00:15:46.440
quite political

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:52.735
at the time, you know, quite social at the time. Cedric wasn't. I mean, I'm not so

00:15:52.735 --> 00:15:53.580
clear as to

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.380
what his position was.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.320
You know, again, he was an extraordinary guy.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.320
You know, he was sort of...

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:08.620
looked sort of like a conservative who was an artist,

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:11.500
if you see what I mean, in terms of his physical appearance

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:12.720
and the way he talked.

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.460
But actually, his political leadings?

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:19.820
Harder to point out.

00:16:20.140 --> 00:16:21.980
Now, I think the other thing that's important

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:24.140
in terms of the deflation that Rem talks about.

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.000
I mean, he was an enfant terrible.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:28.800
I mean, he was a provocateur.

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:30.920
So, you know, that was delightful.

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:32.180
That was great.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:34.400
You know, and as I said before,

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:35.820
I don't think he took himself that seriously,

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:37.740
but he probably did, but didn't.

00:16:38.180 --> 00:16:40.080
So I think he was very good

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:42.480
at taking the sort of pretension

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:44.260
that architects could put onto something

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:45.100
out of the room.

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:49.360
Definitely, delight was part of his agenda.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:50.620
I mean, he was a happy bloke.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:53.820
in fact actually I think the last time I

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:57.060
I saw Cedric I was

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.120
quite upset actually he was

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:01.960
old and he

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:04.540
I can't remember there was sort of an exhibition

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:07.700
near my office near John Street near

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:11.180
Guilford Street where my office is and I walked

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.300
over after the office and I saw him in there and he was

00:17:14.300 --> 00:17:16.980
he was pretty gone I mean he was

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.260
old and so on and Eleanor was sort of getting him in and out

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:21.240
of a taxi and so on.

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:22.680
And it was a very sad thing to see.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:23.140
I mean, obviously,

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:24.180
it's going to happen to all of us.

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:25.760
So I'm not thinking

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:26.700
we're going to get away with it.

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:28.300
But it was sad

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:29.540
because actually Cedric

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:30.660
was beaming all the time.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:31.700
You know, he was a happy bloke.

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:32.940
Like I say, I never saw him angry.

00:17:33.460 --> 00:17:34.820
I mean, I said earlier

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:35.880
that I didn't see him passionate.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:36.660
He was passionate.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.120
You know, he would argue his corner,

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:41.000
but always with humour and lightness.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:41.500
So I think, yes,

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:42.780
delight was partly there.

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:44.240
And maybe, you know,

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:46.220
Rem is again right about that.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:49.880
I think delight was something

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:56.700
that architects weren't so used to expressing when Cedric was around.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:59.300
People took themselves quite seriously, in a sense.

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:05.700
So I think Peter Cook was sort of famous

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:08.680
while I was a student for being a sort of architect of delight.

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:12.280
He was interested in Japan and he'd made friends with Isozaki

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.360
and he talked about layers in Tokyo and so on.

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:19.360
But again, you see, he had a great sense of humour about it,

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:20.920
but it was all very structured.

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:24.940
Cedric Walson, I think Cedric actually did enjoy

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.160
getting up in the morning and doing his own thing.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:29.560
And as I said, I think some people

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.140
had very close relationships with him,

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:33.200
and I think they obviously,

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:35.420
they were enjoyable relationships.

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:38.600
They were never sycophantic,

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:40.800
something we discussed earlier.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:43.620
I think they enjoyed his company.

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.240
He was fun to be with, and I think he enjoyed things.

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.080
He enjoyed drawing. He enjoyed architecture.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:50.980
he enjoyed learning, he enjoyed being here and so on.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:56.940
So yes, I think so, and I think maybe that is something that he's left as a legacy

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:03.040
to quite a number of people, to actually just take architecture off its pedestal

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:04.620
and see it in a different way.

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:11.120
I think it's a very important role, but I would also say that it's also a live role.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:19.405
I think that somehow to think that Cedric was the only character who could do that

00:19:19.405 --> 00:19:20.920
is problematic.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:24.940
I think in a sense there probably are some Cedrics around.

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.260
They certainly won't be called Cedric.

00:19:27.740 --> 00:19:28.940
It's an extraordinary name.

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:37.206
But there are probably some Cedrics around that should equally be given opportunity

00:19:37.206 --> 00:19:43.380
to informally have a tremendous influence on what's happening.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:50.200
And again, Cedric was always, just to use the pun, priceless at doing that.

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:54.720
Because he was always with the big players, but he was never a big player.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.640
So he was there with every world architect.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:19:59.580
and what had he built?

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:00.860
You know, it's a sort of, you know,

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:02.840
if you were going to compare, you know, CVs,

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:05.380
how much did he earn?

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:07.860
You want to compare CVs, you know,

00:20:07.880 --> 00:20:09.340
but there he was, he was always a player

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:11.540
and I think maybe in a sense,

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.080
it's not that there are no Cedrics now,

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:15.520
I think it's that maybe we don't allow enough Cedrics

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:17.360
into the conversation as we used to.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.160
I think the whole education,

00:20:20.440 --> 00:20:22.860
the whole Englishness that I was talking about,

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:25.100
the system of individuality,

00:20:25.280 --> 00:20:26.820
which was absolutely critical,

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:31.880
after the war years, you know, in terms of education and so on, has now changed.

00:20:31.880 --> 00:20:36.480
I think it's more process based and happening elsewhere.

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:38.160
So, you know, I think

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.080
to me, even though I'm not an Englishman,

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:45.720
he was the epitome of an Englishman

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.440
and of a system that allowed the free thinker.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:52.320
Now, some people were very critical of that and would say, you know,

00:20:52.320 --> 00:20:56.280
that's being a dilettante or being, you know, sort of not focused enough.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:02.531
but I think in a way that that worries me so I think no legacy or no evolution of

00:21:02.531 --> 00:21:03.380
what Cedric

00:21:03.380 --> 00:21:08.528
was doing is possible unless you allow the maverick thinker to exist again and I

00:21:08.528 --> 00:21:09.300
think that's

00:21:09.860 --> 00:21:16.028
difficult at the moment I'm not saying it won't change but I think it's things the

00:21:16.028 --> 00:21:16.780
conditions

00:21:16.780 --> 00:21:21.658
have changed so I think his his legacy is what he did and maybe what you guys are

00:21:21.658 --> 00:21:22.200
doing and

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:30.000
what the books will say. But I think my urge towards the future is that I would

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:34.800
have thought he would have wanted you to piece the pieces together in whichever

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:40.960
way you thought fit, rather than the way that they should go together.
